\layout Title

JSWED v2.2.9
\layout Author

John Elliott
\layout Abstract

JSWED is an editor for games based on the 
\emph on 
Jet Set Willy
\emph toggle 
 and 
\emph on 
Manic Miner
\emph toggle 
 engines.
 This document describes how to use it.
\layout Standard
\pagebreak_top \pagebreak_bottom 

\begin_inset LatexCommand \tableofcontents{}

\end_inset 


\layout Section

Changes since previous versions
\layout Subsection

Since 2.2.8, the last stable version
\layout Enumerate

A memory-corruption bug has been fixed, which could cause a crash if the
 game being edited had a start room with a higher number than 64.
\layout Enumerate

Memory accesses have also been checked using the valgrind tool, resulting
 in a number of corrections which should improve stability.
\layout Enumerate

JSW64 games are upgraded to Hacklevel 12, which allows 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Trap
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 cells.
 
\layout Enumerate

If you put Maria and the bathroom toilet in the same room, the game engine
 will be automatically patched to support this.
\layout Enumerate

The cyan highlight is correctly drawn on the JSW64 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

portals
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 screen.
\layout Subsection

Since previous 2.2.
\emph on 
x
\emph default 
 versions
\layout Enumerate

Bug fix: Items were not shown in games based on the JSW64 engine.
\layout Enumerate

Bug fix: Guardians whose sprites were local to a room were not displayed.
\layout Enumerate

Guardians and items are now drawn with transparent backgrounds, rather than
 having a little block of colour follow them about.
\layout Enumerate

A tune import/export facility has been added.
 See section 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sub:musicedit}

\end_inset 

.
\layout Enumerate

More room navigation keys: CTRL+P takes you to the previous room; CTRL+J
 allows you to jump to an arbitrary room number; CTRL+L refreshes the current
 room.
\layout Enumerate

Multiple items can be placed in a single position by holding down SHIFT
 while placing the items.
\layout Enumerate

An autosave facility has been added.
\layout Enumerate

Bug fix: If a portal is using sprites local to the room, then when the sprite
 is changed the portal in the room display will immediately be updated to
 match it.
\layout Enumerate

The Manic Miner and JSW64 cell graphic editors now preview in colour.
 
\layout Enumerate

Bugfix: Preview bitmaps no longer have their last row endlessly repeated.
 
\layout Enumerate

Bugfix: Drawing in the font editor now immediately updates the list view.
 
\layout Enumerate

Bugfix: The sprite chooser for guardians now displays all 8 frames of the
 sprites.
 
\layout Enumerate

Bugfix: A race condition in the guardian editor could cause a crash.
 
\layout Enumerate

Bugfix: Various values for multicolour vertical guardians can now be edited.
 
\layout Enumerate

Bugfix: When switching between two views of the same room, out-of-date data
 (eg.
 guardian paths) were being cached.
 
\layout Enumerate

A hex editor utility has been added, allowing arbitrary changes to be made
 to the internal structure of a game.
\layout Enumerate

The HOME and END keys now have functions.
 HOME takes you to the start of a line of text; END to the end.
 SHIFT+HOME takes you to the top of a scrolling list; SHIFT+END to the end.
\layout Enumerate

The file selector now remembers what its current directory is.
\layout Enumerate

A simple screenshot utility has been added.
 To use it, switch to the 'Rooms' screen and press F8.
 Each room in the game will be saved as a numbered .PNG file in the current
 directory.
\layout Enumerate

Bug fix: Rope positioning now works in JSW64 rooms
\layout Enumerate

Bugfix in the JSW128 to JSW64 conversion, where the target JSW64 game has
 64 rooms.
\layout Enumerate

Sprites can be imported and exported from the sprite editor.JSW128 games
 can be converted to JSW64 format, at the cost of some of their rooms.
\layout Enumerate

Arrows converted from JSW48/128 to JSW64 don't end up as four dots.
\layout Enumerate

WRITETYPER mode works correctly in the V/W variants of JSW64.
 
\layout Enumerate

The title screen editor can export images (in Spectrum SCREEN$ or PNG format)
 and import them (in SCREEN$, PNG, BMP and JPG formats).
\layout Section

Legal Bits
\layout Standard

This version of JSWED is released under the GNU General Public License(sic).
 If you are just using JSWED, this won't affect you; but if you intend to
 distribute copies, then you must distribute all the source code as well
 as the programs.
 See the file COPYING (in the Windows version, COPYING.TXT) for details.
\layout Standard

The Z80 patches (room_*.hex, rtime*bin) which are loaded as part of a JSW64
 game are not covered by the GPL (since this would make it impossible to
 link them with the JSW engine).
 They are released under the zlib/libpng licence.
\layout Section

Installing JSWED 
\layout Subsection

Installing under Windows
\layout Standard

This release of JSWED requires Windows 95, 98, ME, NT, 2000 or XP (and it'll
 probably work on future versions like Vista as well).
 The .ZIP file contains the setup programs; unzip it and run SETUP.EXE.
 
\layout Standard

If you are troubled by frequent crashes, this is probably caused by bugs
 in JSWED.
 However, it may also be an idea to stop JSWED using DirectX, to see if
 this improves stability.
 To disable DirectX under Windows 9x/ME, add the following line to your
 AUTOEXEC.BAT file:
\layout Quote


\family typewriter 
SET SDL_VIDEODRIVER=windib
\layout Standard

and reboot.
 Under Windows NT/2000/XP, you need to add an environment variable, which
 is done using the System control panel.
 Set the new variable's name to 
\family typewriter 
SDL_VIDEODRIVER
\family default 
 and its value to 
\family typewriter 
windib
\family default 
.
\layout Subsection

Installing under 
\noun on 
Unix
\layout Standard

Provided you have the SDL and SDL_image libraries on your system, you should
 just be able to get away with
\layout Standard


\family typewriter 
./configure
\layout Standard


\family typewriter 
make
\layout Standard


\family typewriter 
su root
\layout Standard


\family typewriter 
make install
\layout Subsection

Installing under BeOS
\layout Standard

This should behave the same way as 
\noun on 
Unix
\noun default 
, though you may have to use --prefix= options in the './configure' line.
\layout Section

Running JSWED
\layout Standard

In Windows, double-click JSWED.EXE, start it from the Start menu or change
 to the directory where JSWED is installed and type 
\family typewriter 
jswed 
\emph on 
{filename}
\family default 
\emph default 
 .
 In 
\noun on 
Unix
\noun default 
, type 
\family typewriter 
jswed 
\emph on 
{filename}
\family default 
\emph default 
 at a command prompt, or set up a shortcut to it in your preferred desktop
 environment.
 If 
\emph on 
{filename}
\emph default 
 is present, it should be the name of a file to load.
\layout Standard

When JSWED is started, the main window will appear.
 There will be six menu options:
\layout Subsection

Load file
\layout Standard

Brings up a file selector from which you can choose the file to load.
 JSWED supports +3DOS, TAP, SNA and Z80 formats.
 Note that 128k .SNA files can be saved and loaded by JSWED, but may not
 be compatible with emulators.
 If you intend to use +3DOS files you should read the notes below (section
 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sec: p3dos}

\end_inset 

).
\layout Standard

To change to a different drive (Windows only), tab to the filename field
 at the bottom of the screen, clear out what's in there (Press CTRL+Y) and
 type its drive name, followed by a colon.
 Then press RETURN.
\layout Standard

Once a file is successfully loaded the top line of the screen shows the
 loaded file and its type (48k, 128k, Softricks, Henry's Hoard, etc.).
\layout Subsubsection


\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec: p3dos}

\end_inset 

+3DOS file support
\layout Standard

As far as 48k games go, this is quite easy.
 The file format JSWED uses is a single file, CODE 32768,32768.
 The fun comes with 128k games, because they are stored in multiple +3DOS
 files.
\layout Standard

When loading or saving, JSWED must be given the filename of the BASIC loader
 (
\family typewriter 
JetSet.128
\family default 
).
 The other files that make up JSW128 are assumed to be in the same directory
 as the loader, and have the standard +3DOS filenames for JSW files (
\family typewriter 
rtime.js2
\family default 
, 
\family typewriter 
rooms.js6
\family default 
 etc.) Be especially careful when saving, because files with these names
 will be overwritten without asking.
\layout Standard

The loader program (
\family typewriter 
JetSet.128
\family default 
) saved in +3DOS mode is a 'universal' loader that ought to work on cassette
 or disc.
\layout Subsection

Save file
\layout Standard

Brings up the same sort of file selector, so you can save the game.
 The type of file used will be the same as the one used in loading, unless
 you changed it while editing.
\layout Subsection

Edit game
\layout Standard

This is the heart of JSWED and is described fully in section 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sec: editing}

\end_inset 

.
\layout Subsection

Configure
\layout Standard

This is used to set up the Spectrum emulator used for testing, and the Autosave
 feature.
 There are three fields to enter:
\layout Enumerate

The Spectrum emulator filename; you should enter the full path to the emulator.
 The emulator in question must be able to load .Z80 files.
\layout Enumerate

The Autosave filename.
 This should be the name of a .Z80 snapshot, which will be saved at the specified
 interval.
\layout Enumerate

The Autosave interval, in seconds.
 Enter 0 to disable Autosave completely.
 
\layout Subsection

About JSWED
\layout Standard

Displays the current version, and supported game types.
\layout Subsection

Leave program
\layout Standard

Will leave JSWED instantly.
 Unsaved work will be lost.
 Closing the JSWED window also has this effect.
\layout Section

The user interface
\layout Standard

The JSWED user interface is based on the Spectrum 128 editor, plus features
 from more recent GUIs.
 On most screens, the current control will be highlighted by a blue dotted
 border, and you can use TAB to move from one to the next.
 Text controls contain a blue/white flashing cursor, which can be moved
 by the cursor keys (the control will scroll if there's more text than can
 be seen).
\layout Standard

In the menus, use the cursor keys and ENTER to choose an option; PageUp/PageDown
 allow scrolling menus to move a page at a time.
 Press ESCAPE to cancel the menu.
\layout Part

Jet Set Willy (JSW48 and JSW128)
\layout Section


\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec: editing}

\end_inset 

Editing games
\layout Standard

The game editor is composed of a series of pages, with a list in the top
 left-hand corner.
 To choose a page, click on its title or use ALT+UP / ALT+DOWN to choose
 between them.
\layout Standard

Each page has a context menu, which can be accessed by right-clicking the
 screen or pressing the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

menu
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 key (next to the bottom right-hand CTRL key).
\layout Standard

At any time while editing a game, you can press F2 to save your file, or
 F5 to run it in a Spectrum emulator.
\layout Standard

The screens are:
\layout Subsection

Game
\layout Standard

This page allows you to change the file format used for saving (note that
 128k games saved in SNA format can be reloaded in JSWED, but the 128k SNA
 format may not be compatible with emulators).
 The page also contains a list of patches which can be applied to the game
 engine.
 Each patch is accompanied by a tickbox which is either ticked (the patch
 is applied), empty (the patch can be applied) or shaded (the patch cannot
 be applied).
 
\layout Subsubsection

Upgrading to 128k / Converting to JSW64
\layout Standard

At the bottom of the Game screen is a button.
 In 48k games it is marked 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Upgrade to 128k
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

; in 128k games, it reads 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Convert to JSW64
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

.
 If you click it, it will offer a choice of formats to convert your game
 to:
