\layout Subsubsection


\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec: moving}

\end_inset 

Moving between rooms
\layout Standard

The panel in the bottom left-hand corner contains controls used to move
 between rooms.
 When you go to another room the current room is saved.
\layout Standard

The four arrows (corresponding to CTRL+cursor keys) move you to neighbouring
 rooms in the JSW map.
 The < > signs use the room number sequence, and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Enter no.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 (press J) lets you choose the new room from a menu.
\layout Subsubsection


\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec: exits}

\end_inset 

Editing exits
\layout Standard

When you ask to edit exits, a menu appears showing the current exit assignments.
 Press ESC to leave them as they are, or choose the exit to set.
 If you chose an exit, a list of rooms then appears for you to choose the
 new destination.
\layout Subsubsection


\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec: clipboard}

\end_inset 

Clipboard
\layout Standard

The clipboard allows you the usual copy/cut/paste options, plus the ability
 to clear the room to any of the four cell types and to mirror the structure
 of the room horizontally or vertically.
 Note that mirroring does not alter ramps, conveyors or exit assignments.
\layout Subsubsection


\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec: roomopts}

\end_inset 

Room options
\layout Standard

The room options menu gives you three options: 
\layout Itemize

General: Allows you to set the border colour, room title and (in 128k games)
 Willy's sprite.
\layout Itemize

Shapes: Shows the bitmaps for the seven cell types, and allows you to edit
 them.
 Right-click a bitmap for reflect/rotate/move options, as in the sprite
 editor.
\layout Itemize

Colours: Allows you to edit the colours of the cells.
\layout Subsubsection


\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec: guards}

\end_inset 

Guardians
\layout Standard

If you don't have a guardian currently selected, you are given the option
 of adding another one (assuming, that is, that you don't have eight in
 the room already).
 If you do have a guardian selected, you are also able to delete it from
 the room or to edit it (section 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sec: gedit}

\end_inset 

).
 
\layout Subsubsection


\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec: conveyopts}

\end_inset 

Conveyor options
\layout Standard

Use the conveyor options menu to set four special conveyor types:
\layout Itemize

Ramp left / Ramp right: Turn the ramp into a conveyor ramp (escalator),
 with no separate conveyor.
\layout Itemize

Sticky / off: The conveyor is stationary.
 
\layout Subsubsection


\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec: guardopts}

\end_inset 

Guardian / guardian start frame
\layout Standard

To choose a guardian to work with, click its number in the second menu from
 the right.
 The guardian's eight frames will appear at the bottom of the screen, and
 an outline of its path will be visible at the top.
 To position a guardian, use the cursor or mouse.
 
\layout Standard

To change the guardian's start frame, press ALT+numbers, or click on the
 animations within the guardian list.
\layout Subsubsection

Cell editors
\layout Standard

Rooms in JSW are composed of the four elements known to Greek philosophy
 - Air, Earth, Water and Fire.
 They can also have at most one ramp and one conveyor.
\layout Standard

Edit the layout of the room by pressing A,E,W,F,R or C, moving the cursor
 to where you want, and pressing SPACE or clicking the mouse.
 You can drag the mouse to draw shapes.
\layout Standard

The ramp and conveyor editors need two presses of SPACE (or two clicks),
 one at each end.
 For a ramp, start at the bottom; as you press left/right, the ramp will
 grow.
 For a conveyor, start at the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

from
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 end and move the cursor towards the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

to
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 end.
 If you press SPACE twice without moving in between, the ramp/conveyor will
 be deleted; to create a ramp/conveyor one character long, grow it to two
 characters and then shrink it.
\layout Standard

Items are superimposed on the other cell types.
 Select the item type by pressing 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

I
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

.
 To add an item, click where one is not; to remove it, click where one is.
\layout Subsection

Teleport editor
\layout Standard

The teleport overlay is supported for JSW48, JSW128 and JSW64 games.
 By default teleportation is not supported; to get teleporter support you
 must load the overlay.
 In JSW48 you then have to sacrifice a room to the teleporter code; in JSW128/JS
W64 it is allocated from the memory map.
\layout Standard

Once the teleporter overlay is loaded, you can click on the room to create
 a teleporter (which will appear as an orange 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

T
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

).
 You will then be asked to choose its destination.
\layout Standard

If you click on an existing teleporter, you can delete it or change its
 destination.
\layout Subsection

Start position editor
\layout Standard

This editor allows you to position three special sprites - Willy's initial
 position, Maria and the toilet.
 Click the sprites (or press W / M / T) and then position them in the room
 you want.
 Note that in Henry's Hoard you can only position Willy/Henry.
 In a Geoff Mode game you can't have Maria and the toilet in the same room;
 in a normal game, you can.
 
\layout Standard

If you have the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

No infinite death
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 patch turned on, there will be a fourth special sprite - the safe restart
 position (a green version of Willy).
 This must be set to a valid value for each room in the game; if Willy dies
 in a room where this position has not been set, the game will crash!
\layout Subsection


\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec: fontedit}

\end_inset 

Font editor
\layout Standard

The font editor works exactly like the sprite editor - click on a letter,
 and edit its bitmap.
\layout Subsection

Screen editor
\layout Standard

The screen editor works like the room editor - use cursor keys or the mouse
 to move the cursor within the image, and press SPACE or click the mouse
 to draw.
 Depending on which screen you are editing, you may be able to edit just
 the pixels, just the attributes, or both.
 Press the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

M
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 key or click the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Mode
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 icon to switch between pixel and attribute modes.
\layout Subsubsection

The Zoom window
\layout Standard

To the left of the main screen window is a zoom window.
 This shows a scaled-up version of the character cell the cursor is currently
 in.
 Clicks in this window have no effect; it's for viewing only.
\layout Subsubsection

Editing pixels
\layout Standard

Click on a pixel to toggle it between ink and paper; you can drag the mouse
 to set several pixels to the same colour.
 The keyboard control may be more useful here because moving the mouse by
 one pixel at a time is quite fiddly.
\layout Subsubsection

Editing attributes
\layout Standard

The current attribute that will be used is set using I(nk), P(aper), B(right),
 F(lash).
 Once you have chosen the attribute combination you want, click on a character
 square to set its colours.
\layout Subsubsection

Grids
\layout Standard

Press 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

G
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 or click on the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Grid
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 icon to switch between the two available grids.
 They are the Pixel grid (attributes show as normal, but a pattern of squares
 appears instead of the pixels) and the Attribute grid (pixels show as normal,
 but the attributes are a fixed pattern).
 
\layout Subsubsection

Next / Previous
\layout Standard

Press 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

<
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 / 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

>
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 or click the same symbols onscreen to move from one picture to the next.
 The following pictures exist in the different games:
\layout Standard
