\layout Subsubsection

Undo
\layout Standard

Press Control-Z or Control-U to undo everything you have done to a picture
 since the last 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

<
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

>
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

.
 Press Control-Z again to undo the undo.
\layout Subsubsection

Import / Export
\layout Standard

Right-click on the screen being edited and you are given the option to import
 or export the current image.
 Images can be exported as PNG or Spectrum SCREEN$; they can be imported
 as SCREEN$, PNG, BMP or JPG.
 
\layout Standard

Trying to import an image for a screen where you can only edit the attributes
 (for example, the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

bottom third
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 graphic) will generate an imported screen, and then load only that screen's
 attributes.
\layout Subsection


\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sub:musicedit}

\end_inset 

The Music editor
\layout Standard

The music editor is present only in 128k games (JSW128 and JSW64).
 It allows the three tunes to be played, exported or imported.
 Under the description of each tune are three buttons:
\layout Description

Test runs the game in an emulator, just as pressing F5 does; except that
 the title screen tune is changed to be the tune you want to test.
\layout Description

Export saves the selected tune.
 Two formats are supported: +3DOS (this just saves the tune as a block of
 memory) and Assembly (a text format, described more fully below).
\layout Description

Import loads a new tune over the currently selected one.
 Both formats are supported.
 If the new tune won't fit in the space available, the import will fail.
 JSWED will automatically fix-up the file as it loads, so you can load a
 tune into a slot other than the one it was saved from.
\layout Subsubsection

The Assembly file format
\layout Standard

The Assembly file format is the most suitable format for editing a tune
 file or creating a new one (though that's not saying much).
 It's in the form of an assembly language listing, compatible with Udo Munk's
 Z80ASM assembler.
 The only directives used (and the only ones that will be recognised on
 import) are:
\layout LyX-Code

org  defw  defb  end
\layout Standard

A tune specified in Assembly format should be laid out as follows:
\layout LyX-Code

\layout LyX-Code

        org     0f700h
\layout Standard

The org (origin) directive should be the first one in the file.
 The value following it should be greater than 4000h but is otherwise comparativ
ely unimportant.
 In the case of tunes saved by JSWED, it gives the address where the tune
 was saved from.
\layout Standard

The next three directives must be the addresses of the three channels:
\layout LyX-Code

        defw    Gf706
\layout LyX-Code

        defw    Gf751
\layout LyX-Code

        defw    Gf752 
\layout Standard

Usually these are stored as label names, but you could use numerical addresses
 if you wanted.
 JSWED outputs labels of the form Gxxxx, but you might want to use more
 memorable names - eg:
\layout LyX-Code

        defw    channel1
\layout LyX-Code

        defw    channel2
\layout LyX-Code

        defw    channel3
\layout Standard

Following the channel addresses, the remainder of the file describes the
 tune data.
 For example:
\layout LyX-Code

GF706:  defb    0FEh    ;Call subroutine
\layout LyX-Code

        defw    Gf732
\layout LyX-Code

        defb    0FEh    ;Call subroutine
\layout LyX-Code

        defw    Gf742
\layout LyX-Code

        defb    03eh,00ah
\layout LyX-Code

        defb    03bh,00ah
\layout LyX-Code

        defb    03eh,014h
\layout LyX-Code

        defb    03dh,028h 
\layout Standard

The first line in the listing has a label.
 You can put a label on any line of the file, but it only makes sense to
 put them at the start of channels and at the start of subroutines.
\layout Standard

Each channel is formed of a series of instructions, constructed using defb
 and defw directives.
 The first byte is a command byte, which may then be followed by parameter
 bytes.
 These can be on the same line or on different lines; JSWED doesn't care.
\layout Standard

Any text on a line after a ; is ignored.
\layout LyX-Code

        end
\layout Standard

JSWED's output files end with the 'end' directive, but this is optional.
\layout Standard


