\layout Subsection

Guardian types
\layout Standard

In JSW48, there are four guardian types - horizontal, vertical, arrow and
 rope.
 In JSW128 there are ten; the four mentioned earlier, two diagonal types,
 and flashing versions of all except the arrow and the rope.
 In general JSWED tries to preserve guardian data when changing from one
 type to another, but this is not always possible; so changing from a vertical
 guardian to a rope and back won't give you the guardian you had before.
\layout Standard

In Geoff Mode, there are four guardian types (horizontal/diagonal, vertical,
 arrow and rope).
 Use the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

H/D
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 setting to switch between horizontal and diagonal.
\layout Subsection

Editing conventional guardians
\layout Standard

There are no real pitfalls with editing conventional guardians.
 If for some reason the preview of the guardian's bounding rectangle gets
 out of step with the values you are entering, it is possible to force a
 recalculation.
 To do this, click [!] for the hex editor, and then click [OK] to dismiss
 it - don't change any of the fields!
\layout Subsection

Rope editing
\layout Standard

The initial position of the rope is positive if the rope starts in the right-han
d half of the screen, negative if the rope starts in the left-hand half,
 and zero if the rope starts dead centre.
 It should be less than the rope 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

swing
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 value.
\layout Standard

The rope length can be 1-32; 32 is the usual value.
\layout Standard

Unless you are using the (new) 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

adjacent ropes
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 patch, ropes must be stored last in a room, 
\emph on 
or
\emph toggle 
 be followed by an arrow, or by an instance of a blank guardian such as
 Guardian 0.
 This is because a bug (or feature?) in the game engine causes a rope to
 write bytes into the following guardian's data.
\layout Subsection

Arrow editing
\layout Standard

Arrows have 256 possible positions.
 Numbers 0-31 are onscreen; the others are offscreen.
 This is set up by the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

start pos
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 field.
 The small bitmap editor above it is used to set the pattern used on the
 top and bottom of the arrow.
\layout Subsection


\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec: hexedit}

\end_inset 

Hex editing
\layout Standard

Press the [!] button to edit a guardian as hexadecimal bytes.
 It is possible to create guardians that crash JSW by doing this, so take
 care.
\layout Subsection

Geoff Mode bounds
\layout Standard

In Geoff Mode, the guardian bounds are, respectively, an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

initial frame count
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 and a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

maximum frame count
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

, rather than 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

left limit
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

right limit
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

.
\layout Section

Good Advice 
\layout Standard

The following notes are based on the documentation of Paul Rhodes's JSW
 editor, with additional suggestions by Andrew Broad.
 Note that if you have the 'Black Willy' patch applied, any reference to
 'white' below should be read as 'black'.
\layout Enumerate

Don't put anything (except items) in the path of a guardian.
 Guardian collisions are pixel based; you have to be sure that none of the
 guardian's pixels touches any pixels already on the screen.
\layout Enumerate

Don't have arrows passing through anything with white INK.
 
\layout Enumerate

If you have white background INK, any items in the room on background cells
 will be automatically collected on entry to the room (see swimming pool).
 
\layout Enumerate

Be careful if you have two cell types with the same attributes.
 In particular, don't make other cells the same colour as Fire, and don't
 make Fire the same as Air but with white ink.
\layout Enumerate

Having the same attributes for ramp and conveyor creates an escalator (see
 Chapel).
 Other combinations that may be useful are giving Water or Earth the same
 attributes as the ramp or conveyor.
\layout Enumerate

Avoid having Fire cells at the top of the screen above a gap at the bottom
 (see right-hand half of Under the Roof).
 
\layout Enumerate

Do not put anything in the path of a rope (try it and see!), and do not
 allow an arrow to pass through a rope (especially not a white rope).
 Note that putting the arrow 
\emph on 
after
\emph default 
 the rope in the room's guardian instance list is OK - but putting it 
\emph on 
before
\emph default 
 the rope results in a collision.
\layout Enumerate

Never allow guardians to collide.
 Their paths can overlap if you make sure that only one guardian enters
 the overlapping space at any one time.
 
\layout Enumerate

Objects on top of other cell types (floors, walls, etc.) cannot be collected
 by Willy.
 They can be collected by being touched with white arrows or white guardians.
\layout Enumerate

It is possible for a JSW game to contain infinite-death scenarios, where
 Willy loses all his lives and you can do nothing about it.
 For example, jumping off the end of the upper platform in The Front Door
 does this.
 Try to stop these occurring unless the player deliberately does something
 suicidal (as in the above example).
 
\layout Enumerate

Quirky features are a pathway to many abilities some consider to be unnatural.
 Learn to recognise them, and you'll be able to spot any unintended loopholes
 in games.
 Learn to use them, and you will achieve a power far greater than any Jedi.
\layout Enumerate

Playtest every room thoroughly, especially all possible exits from the room.
 If you make even a trivial change to a room, never forget to playtest it
 again.
\layout Enumerate

Do a complete playtest to make sure the final revision of the game is fully
 completable (to the point where Willy sticks his head down the toilet)
 before you release it.
\layout Enumerate

Save your work regularly by pressing F2.
\layout Enumerate

Hard-drives fail, floppy disks get corrupted, CDs become unreadable, buildings
 burn down and files uploaded to the Internet get removed, but if you regularly
 save your files in three completely different places, then the probability
 of losing your game is very slim.
\layout Part


\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec: mmediting}

\end_inset 

Manic Miner
\layout Section

Editing Games
\layout Standard

The game editor behaves in the same way as the Jet Set Willy one, but with
 fewer pages.
 
\layout Subsection

Game
\layout Standard

This page acts like the JSW 'Game' page, but there is only one patch - 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Specials by room
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

.
 This allows each room to control whether it has vertical guardians, skylabs,
 solar power, Kong or Eugene (instead of these options being hard-coded).
\layout Subsection

Message
\layout Standard

This page contains messages printed by the game - the title screen message,
 the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Game Over
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 message, and the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

score
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 message.
 You can edit them as you like; the program will not allow you to enter
 more text than will fit.
\layout Subsection

Sprites
\layout Standard

The sprite editor screen is divided into two parts.
 The bottom half contains a list of sprites; select these using the left
 and right cursor keys (use PageUp/PageDown to move faster) and click or
 press ENTER on the chosen sprite.
 You can also scroll the list with the mouse, by clicking on the arrows
 in the bottom corners.
\layout Standard

Once you have chosen a sprite, switch to the editor using TAB and use the
 four cursor keys to move the cursor, SPACE to toggle the cell colour (or
 use the mouse).
 The context menu for this screen allows you to reflect, rotate, move, invert
 and clear the bitmap.
\layout Standard

Press CTRL+Z or CTRL+U to undo changes to the current sprite.
 Press it again to undo the undo.
\layout Standard

Note that this only covers Willy and guardian sprites, not portals, Eugene
 or other special graphics.
\layout Subsection

The Room Editor
\layout Standard
\added_space_bottom 0.3cm 
The room editor is similar to the JSW version, but the following functions
 are different:
\layout Subsubsection

Moving between rooms
\layout Standard

Since Manic Miner has no exits at the edges of rooms, there are no arrows
 to move between rooms.
 Likewise, there is no option to set up the exits.
\layout Subsubsection

Guardians
\layout Standard

The guardians are set up on a separate screen - see section 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{mmguard}

\end_inset 

.
\layout Subsubsection

Editing cells
\layout Standard

In the JSW room editor, there are two options on the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Options
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 menu - 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Shapes
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Colours
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

.
 In the Manic Miner editor, these are combined as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Shapes
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

.
 The Manic Miner shape editor only shows one of the cell types at a time;
 use the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

<
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

>
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 buttons to see the others.
\layout Standard

Manic Miner supports more cell types than Jet Set Willy's 4-element system.
 It allows:
\layout Itemize

Air
\layout Itemize

Water
\layout Itemize

Water 2: If the room has a Kong beast, this is used for switches.
 Otherwise it's another type of Water cell.
\layout Itemize

Crumbly - disintegrates when Willy walks on it.
\layout Itemize

Earth
\layout Itemize

Convey - animated conveyor.
 You can only have one animaetd conveyor in a room.
\layout Itemize

Conv 2 - non-animated conveyor.
 You can have as many cells of this type as you like.
\layout Itemize

Fire
\layout Itemize

Fire 2
\layout Itemize

? - user-defined attribute.
 Not normally useful.
\layout Subsubsection

Room options
\layout Standard

Selecting 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Room options
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 takes you straight to the room property screen.
 You can set the border colour and the amount of air remaining.
\layout Itemize

General: Allows you to set the border colour, room title and air remaining.
\layout Itemize

Shapes: Shows the bitmaps and colours for the eight cell types, and allows
 you to edit them.
 Right-click a bitmap for reflect/rotate/move options, as in the sprite
 editor.
\layout Subsubsection

Conveyor options
\layout Standard

Use the conveyor options menu to set the conveyor to be Off or Sticky.
 It also allows you to move the conveyor animation up and down.
\layout Subsubsection

Item options
\layout Standard

Use this screen to design the item graphic, and to set the colours of the
 up to five items in the room.
 The item graphic will be the same for all of them.
\layout Subsection


\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{mmguard}

\end_inset 

Guardians page
\layout Standard

The guardians page is used to position guardians within a room.
 Select the guardian you want from the list at the bottom of the screen,
 and then you can set its start position using the mouse (or cursor keys
 plus space).
\layout Standard

Right-click to add a new guardian, or to edit or delete the currently selected
 one.
 
\layout Subsection

Portal editor
\layout Standard

This editor allows you to edit Willy's start position in each room, and
 the portal which takes you to the next room.
 If the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

specials by room
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 patch has been applied, then you can also control whether the room has
