** Although there are 256 cell types, only 13 of them are 'real'; the others
 behave like differently-coloured water cells.
 
\layout Section

Memory Map
\layout Standard

JSW64 does not have guardian tables.
 Otherwise this screen is identical to the JSW128 one.
\layout Section

Rooms
\layout Standard

As with the Manic Miner editor, the guardians have been moved to a page
 of their own.
 Other differences are:
\layout Subsection

Cells
\layout Standard

In Manic Miner, JSW48 and JSW128, the meaning of cells is fixed - the first
 cell is air, the second is water, and so on.
 In JSW64, this is controlled by the game itself.
 In the V and X variants, the cell types are set up by room; so one room
 could have three types of 'fire' cell and no conveyors, while another could
 have one fire, and two conveyors (one going each way).
 In the other four variants, the cell type is set up globally; your choice
 of cell behaviour affects all rooms.
\layout Standard

Because of this definable behaviour, the room editor doesn't use shortcuts
 like A=Air, W=Water, E=Earth and so on.
 It uses 0,1,2,3 and so on to choose the first,second,third...
 pattern.
 Ramps and conveyors are just normal cell types.
\layout Standard

As with Manic Miner, the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

shapes
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

colours
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 screen have been combined - this time into a screen called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Cells
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

.
 This handles both shapes and colours.
 It also has a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Type
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 control which can be used to set the behaviour of each cell.
 Remember that in variants other than V and X, changing a cell type affects
 every room in the game.
\layout Subsubsection

Extra cell types
\layout Standard

JSW64 has the following cell types:
\layout Description

Air/Water/Earth/Fire behave like their JSW48 / JSW128 counterparts.
\layout Description

Ramps\SpecialChar ~
and\SpecialChar ~
conveyors are now normal cells rather than one-offs.
 You can therefore have as many of either as you want in a room.
\layout Description

Crumbling\SpecialChar ~
blocks behave as in Manic Miner.
\layout Description

Trampolines are an addition from the Dragon 32 version of JSW.
 When Willy stands on one, he is forced to jump.
\layout Description

Traps are based on Andrew Broad's 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

harmless fire
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 patch.
 If any part of Willy is above a trap, he falls.
 Traps should be used with caution; letting Willy jump up onto a trap from
 below can have unexpected consequences.
\layout Standard

The editor also lets you position three other 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

cell
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 types:
\layout Description

Item lets you position items, exactly as in JSW48 / JSW128.
\layout Description

Solar is used to set the start point of the Solar Power beam (if the room
 has solar power).
 The start position of the beam is indicated by an orange sun.
 Note that the editor will only let you put the sun in the top half of the
 room.
 You must also set colours for the solar beam in the room properties screen
 before positioning the sun; to remove it, set its colours to black on black.
\layout Description

Custom (Variant Z only): Place any combination of foreground and background
 attributes.
 These will be drawn with the pattern of cell 1 (cell 0 is usually used
 for air and therefore is unlikely to have a pattern).
 In the game, custom cells will behave as Water.
\layout Subsection

Room properties
\layout Standard

The 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Room properties
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 screen has a few more options than the JSW48 / JSW128 one.
 These are:
\layout Description

Willy Set Willy's colour.
\layout Description

Solar Set the colours for the Solar Power beam.
 If you don't want a room to have solar power, set these to black on black,
 with Bright and Flash unticked.
\layout Description

Air The amount of air in the room.
 162 (the maximum value) means 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

air is not limited
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

.
\layout Description

Rigor\SpecialChar ~
Mortis If this is ticked, guardians in the room will not move until
 all the items in the room are collected.
\layout Description

No\SpecialChar ~
Kamikaze If this is ticked, when Willy dies he loses any items he collected
 since entering the room.
 This is intended for compatibility with Manic Miner-style rooms, but a
 sadistic game designer may decide to use it in other circumstances.
\layout Description

Bonus\SpecialChar ~
Room If this is ticked, each item collected gives Willy an extra life.
  
\layout Description

Escalator Tick this to make all ramps in the room into escalators.
 There is a second tickbox to control whether you want them to be 'up' or
 'down' escalators.
 
\layout Description

Land\SpecialChar ~
softly (aka 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Fall any height
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

).
 If this is ticked, Willy cannot die through falling (in this room).
\layout Subsection

Room-specific graphics
\layout Standard

JSW64 variants allow some space in the room definition to be used for 16x16
 graphics (eg, for a portal).
 Variants X,Y,Z and [ can store a complete set of frames (eg, for a custom
 sprite for Willy).
\layout Standard

To edit these sprites, choose 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Sprites
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 from the room options menu.
 You can then edit the sprites in the room, in the same way that you can
 edit the systemwide sprites.
\layout Subsection

Conveyor animation
\layout Standard

As in Manic Miner, the conveyor animation in JSW64 games is independent
 of the actual conveyor.
 Unlike when you are editing a Manic Miner game, drawing conveyor cells
 does not update the animation (or vice versa).
 You have to do the animation separately; press V and then draw the animation
 over the blocks.
 With this system it's quite possible to have a conveyor that goes in one
 direction and animates in the other!
\layout Subsection

Final Barrier
\layout Standard

JSW64 contains two graphical buffers that can be used to draw particular
 levels (as the title screen is used in Manic Miner to draw the Final Barrier).
 To set a room to use one or other of these buffers (or with the top/bottom
 half of the title screen), select 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Final Barrier
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 from the room options menu.
 A screen will then appear showing the room, with two lists.
 One selects the buffer to use for the top half of the room; the other selects
 the bottom.
\layout Section

Guardians
\layout Standard

In JSW64, as in Manic Miner, guardians are set up individually in each room.
 The number of guardian slots available varies depending on the JSW variant
 you are using, and can be further customised by room.
\layout Standard

The guardian choice screen shows a list of guardian slots; each one is shown
 as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Grd
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 if there is a guardian in it, or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

--
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 if it is empty.
 Click on the screen to set the start position of the guardian; right-click
 to get the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Guardians
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 menu.
 The start frame is set in the guardian editor rather than using ALT+1 -
 ALT+8.
\layout Standard

Unlike in JSW48 / JSW128, guardians in JSW64 are not shared between rooms.
 
\layout Subsection

Guardian editor
\layout Standard

The guardian editor behaves just like the JSW128 one, with these exceptions:
\layout Itemize

There are two 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Animation
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 fields.
 The left-hand one is the start frame of the guardian.
\layout Itemize

The arrow editor allows you to set the arrow colour, and the bitmap pattern
 used by the middle of the arrow.
\layout Itemize

Extra guardian types are supported: Skylabs, Angry Eugenes, Triggers, Switches
 and Vanishing Walls.
\layout Subsubsection


\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sub:skylab64}

\end_inset 

Skylabs
\layout Standard

A Skylab behaves like a vertical guardian, except that it does not animate
 until it reaches the end of its travel.
 At this point, it starts again at the beginning, 8 columns to the right.
\layout Standard

The Skylabs are based on those in Manic Miner.
 However, these Skylabs can also go up.
 If so, the first bound is the bottom bound and the second bound is the
 top (the other way round from all other vertical guardians).
\layout Subsubsection

Angry Eugenes
\layout Standard

Angry Eugene behaves like a vertical guardian until his Y-coordinate equals
 either of its bounds (it is therefore usual to set both bounds to the same
 value).
 Once Eugene has reached his destination, he stops.
\layout Standard

Be warned: If the bound value cannot be reached exactly (eg: Y-coordinate
 is 4, bounds are both 50, speed is 4) then Eugene will just keep going,
 probably colliding with something undesirable.
\layout Standard

There are three Eugene types - normal, multicolour, and multicolour 2.
 The second multicolour option exactly duplicates the way Manic Miner does
 a multicoloured Eugene; it will only work in a room with a limited air
 supply.
\layout Subsubsection

Triggers
\layout Standard

A trigger is used to modify the behaviour of the subsequent guardian in
 the table.
 When you edit a trigger, you will see two buttons:
\layout Description

Source is the event that causes the trigger to activate.
 It can be one of:
\begin_deeper 
\layout Itemize

Willy collecting the last item in the room.
\layout Itemize

An angry Eugene stopping.
\layout Itemize

A switch being turned on.
\layout Itemize

A wall vanishing.
\end_deeper 
\layout Description

Effect brings up another guardian editor, in which you can decide exactly
 what will happen when the trigger takes effect.
 The trigger can only change bytes 0 and 4-7 of a guardian; so you can't
 change position, colour or animation settings.
 
\layout Standard

If the trigger is last in the guardian list, then a bright red warning will
 be displayed on the guardian editor.
 If you save a game with a trigger at the end of the list, then the game
 will probably crash when the trigger is activated.
\layout Subsubsection

Switches
\layout Standard

A switch is a guardian, but it's harmless for Willy to touch it.
 At any time, it is turned off or on; if Willy touches it, he may change
 its state.
\layout Standard

By itself, a switch just sits there.
 But other guardians can monitor the state of a switch and take action based
 on its setting.
\layout Standard

The options when editing a switch are:
\layout Description

Sprite The switch graphic.
 Unlike the other guardians, the switch is only 8 pixels high (it is still
 16 pixels wide).
 The top half of its sprite graphic is the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

off
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 image; the bottom half is the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

on
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 image.
\layout Description

Colour/Bright as normal.
\layout Description

Animation chooses which sprite in the page will draw the switch.
\layout Description

On/Off sets the initial state of the switch
\layout Description

Stays\SpecialChar ~
off If this is ticked, then once the switch is turned off it can't
 be turned on.
\layout Description

Stays\SpecialChar ~
on If this is ticked, then once the switch is turned on it can't be
 turned off.
\layout Standard

If you leave both the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Stays
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 options unticked, then while Willy is touching the switch it will rapidly
 alternate between states.
 If both are ticked the switch becomes a useless ornament.
\layout Subsubsection

Vanishing Walls
\layout Standard

A vanishing wall section, when activated, removes a section of the room
 - one character wide, and an arbitrary height.
 Like the trigger, it only activates after another event has taken place,
 and the same 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Source
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

 button is used to select this.
 The other control on the screen - height - selects how many wall pixels
 will be removed.
 
\layout Standard

It is possible to use a vanishing wall as a delay system, by putting it
 on top of air cells.
 It then has no visible effect, but still completes a fixed time after it
 was started.
 
\layout Subsubsection

Stoppers
\layout Standard

Guardians which appear in the list after a stopper do not appear in the
 game.
 The intention is to have a trigger followed by a stopper; when the trigger
 is activated, the stopper is changed to a blank guardian - at which point
 the guardians after the stopper are drawn.
\layout Standard

There are two types of stopper.
 The first one inhibits movement and drawing, so that the extra guards aren't
 visible.
 The second one only inhibits movement - so you can have a 'rigor mortis'-like
 effect on only some guardians in a room.
\layout Subsection

Guardian table
\layout Standard

In JSW64, a room's guardian list can have a variable number of slots.
 To amend this (either to increase the number of guardians in the room,
 or to decrease it and use the memory for something else), right-click in
 the guardian choice screen and select 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Table
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

.
 You will then see a memory map of the room, coloured as follows:
\layout Description

Red Guardian table
\layout Description

Green Free space
\layout Description

Blue Used for other purposes
\layout Description

Cyan Used for sprites
\layout Standard

There is also a +/- control which adjusts the number of slots in the guardian
 table.
 As you alter this, the memory map will be updated to match.
\layout Standard

If you change the size or position of a room's guardian table, cross-references
 in triggers and opening walls will become incorrect and must be set up
 again.
 
\layout Section

Teleport editor
\layout Standard

This works exactly like the JSW48 / JSW128 teleport editor, with one extension.
 From the right-click menu, you can choose to have a visual effect when
 Willy enters a teleporter:
\layout Description

No\SpecialChar ~
special\SpecialChar ~
effect behaves like JSW48 and JSW128; teleporters act as a jump-cut.
 This is the proper setting to use if you are using teleporters to simulate
 switches.
\layout Description

Screen\SpecialChar ~
flashes makes the screen fade to dark blue (as it does when you enter
 a portal in Manic Miner).
\layout Description

Air\SpecialChar ~
runs\SpecialChar ~
down makes the air remaining decrease to zero.
 This has no effect if the room doesn't have a limited air supply.
\layout Description

Both\SpecialChar ~
the\SpecialChar ~
above does both, one after the other - like a true portal.
\layout Section

Start position / Portal editor
\layout Standard

This editor is similar to the JSW48 / JSW128 one, but it also allows you
 to add a portal to each room.
 Portals work the same way as in Manic Miner; they're inactive until Willy
 has collected all the items in the room, and then he can step into one
 and arrive anywhere else in the game.
 Here's a feature comparison:
