\layout Standard

Keypresses you can use here are:
\layout Description

B Set whether portal is bright.
\layout Description

D Set the portal destination.
 You will first be asked for the room; then you can place Willy's arrival
 position within it.
 
\layout Description

F Set whether portal flashes.
 If the portal is flashing Willy can go to the next level whether or not
 he has collected all the items in this room.
\layout Description

G Choose the sprite to use for the portal.
\layout Description

I Set the ink colour for the portal.
\layout Description

M Set the position of Maria.
\layout Description

P Set the paper colour for the portal.
\layout Description

T Set the position of the toilet.
 Note that you can't have Maria and the toilet in the same room.
\layout Description

W Set Willy's start position.
\layout Description

X Set the position of the portal.
\layout Standard

To remove a portal from a room, press G and select 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

No portal
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

.
 If you're adding a portal, it's advisable to set the colour before choosing
 the sprite.
\layout Subsection

Portal special effect
\layout Standard

As with teleporters, it's possible to set the special effect that occurs
 when you enter a portal.
 To choose the effect, right-click and select 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset 

Portal effect
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset 

.
 It can be one of:
\layout Description

No\SpecialChar ~
effect Willy is instantly transported to the portal destination.
\layout Description

Screen\SpecialChar ~
flashes The screen fades to blue but the air (if limited) does not
 run down.
\layout Description

Screen\SpecialChar ~
flash\SpecialChar ~
+\SpecialChar ~
air The screen fades and the air runs down.
\layout Standard

The portal effect is set by room, rather than systemwide.
\layout Section

JSW64 Good advice
\layout Itemize

If Willy stands on a trampoline in a room with Superjump turned on, he will
 shoot straight up until he either:
\begin_deeper 
\layout Enumerate

Arrives in the room above;
\layout Enumerate

Loses a life;
\layout Enumerate

Hits something solid.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout Itemize

Make sure the Solar Power beam doesn't go off the screen, or the game engine
 will crash.
 Block it with Earth if necessary.
\layout Itemize

Unlike in JSW48 and JSW128, you can't combine the behaviour of different
 cells by setting them to the same attribute.
\layout Section

JSW64 for machine-code programmers
\layout Standard

The internal data format and mechanisms of JSW48 are very well known (which
 is one reason why there are many more JSW editors than, for example, Mutant
 Monty editors).
 This section is an attempt to document JSW64 to a similar level of detail.
 
\layout Subsection

JSW64 Room Formats
\layout Standard

Notes:
\layout Enumerate

In the format description below, fields marked 'reserved' may be used by
 future versions of the JSW64 engine.
 Fields marked 'unused' will not; they are intended for your own data.
\layout Enumerate

For more information on the Manic Miner 'Air' and 'Portal' features, see
 Andrew Broad's Manic Miner room format at <http://www.geocities.com/andrewbroad/s
pectrum/willy/mm_format.html>
\layout Standard

Formats W, Y and [ use a 4-bit room map, so they can all specify 16 block
 types.
 But rooms in W and Y only have space for 13 block types, so the other three
 are defined globally (in bank 7).
\layout Subsubsection

Bytes 00-B5 for formats V and X
\layout Description

00-68 Up to 13 guardian instances for the room, terminated with 0FFh.
\layout Description

69-6D Meanings of the following 8 cell types.
 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard

First byte low nybble = type of first cell
\layout Standard

First byte high nybble = type of second cell
\layout Standard

2nd byte low nybble = type of third cell
\layout Standard

etc.
\end_deeper 
\layout Description

6E-B5 Attributes and bitmap for the 8 cell types
\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard

Cell types are:
\layout Description

0 Air
\layout Description

1 Water
\layout Description

2 Earth
\layout Description

3 Fire
\layout Description

4 Ramp 
\backslash 

\layout Description

5 Ramp /
\layout Description

6 Conveyor <<
\layout Description

7 Conveyor >>
\layout Description

8 Crumbling floor
\layout Description

9 Trampoline
\layout Description

10-15 reserved.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout Subsubsection

Bytes 00-B5 for formats W, Y and Z
\layout Description

00-40 Up to 8 guardian instances for the room, terminated with 0FFh.
\layout Description

41-B5 Attributes and bitmap for 13 cell types
\layout Subsubsection

Bytes 00-B5 for format [
\layout Description

00-20 Up to 4 guardian instances for the room, terminated with 0FFh.
\layout Description

21-25 Reserved.
\layout Description

26-B5 Attributes and bitmap for 16 cell types
\layout Subsubsection

Bytes B5-FF (all formats)
\layout Description

B6-D5 The room name, ASCII.
\layout Description

D6 Conveyor animation, and the Final Barrier flags.
\begin_deeper 
\layout Description

Bits\SpecialChar ~
5,6,7: Source of bottom half
\layout Description

Bits\SpecialChar ~
2,3,4: Source of top half
\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard

Sources are:
\layout Description

0: Normal
\layout Description

1: Title screen, top half
\layout Description

2: Title screen, bottom half
\layout Description

3: Screen buffer 1
\layout Description

4: Screen buffer 2
\layout Description

5,6,7: Reserved.
\end_deeper 
\layout Description

Bit\SpecialChar ~
1,0: Conveyor settings
\begin_deeper 
\layout Description

0: Animate left
\layout Description

1: Animate right
\layout Description

2: All conveyors in room deactivated
\layout Description

3: All conveyors in room are sticky 
\end_deeper 
\end_deeper 
\layout Description

D7-D8 Conveyor animation position
\layout Description

D9 Conveyor animation length
\layout Description

DA-DB Address of the guardian instance table used by this room.
 Normally 8000h, but if a larger guardian instance table is needed it will
 be the address of that table, either in the room record or elsewhere in
 memory.
 The original guardian table will then be available for your own purposes.
\layout Description

DC Solar Power beam entry point.
 Low 5 bits are X-coordinate; high 3 bits are Y-coordinate (solar power
 must start in the top 8 lines).
 
\layout Description

DD Attribute of the solar beam.
 If this is zero, the room has no solar power.
 Note that unlike in Manic Miner, the solar beam does not require a green
 background.
 If you want a harmless solar beam, you'll have to set the room not to limit
 air supply.
\layout Description

DE Border and flags
\begin_deeper 
\layout Description

Bits\SpecialChar ~
0-2: Border
\layout Description

Bits\SpecialChar ~
3-5: Willy's colour
\layout Description

Bit\SpecialChar ~
6: Rigor Mortis.
 If set, guardians are stationary until all objects in the room have been
 collected.
\layout Description

Bit\SpecialChar ~
7: Superjump.
\end_deeper 
\layout Description

DF Air supply, major value.
 See Andrew Broad's Manic Miner room format for full details of this field.
\layout Description

E0 Air supply, minor value.
 Must be a multiple of 4, or 0xFF if the room does not have a limited air
 supply.
\layout Description

E1 Item bitmap
\layout Description

E9-EC Exits
\layout Description

ED Willy's sprite page.
 If less than 80h, uses the standard Willy.
\layout Description

EE-EF The address of the 16x16 graphic to use for the portal.
 Set to 0 if this room does not have a portal.
\layout Description

F0-F1 Position of portal attribute (based at 5C00h).
\layout Description

F2-F3 Position of portal bitmap (based at 6000h).
 
\layout Description

F4 Portal attribute.
 If it is flashing the portal can be entered even if there are still items
 to be collected.
\layout Description

F5 Portal destination room number.
\layout Description

F6-F7 Portal destination: Position of Willy attributes (based at 5C00h).
 
\layout Description

F8 Portal destination: Willy's new Y-coordinate (based at 6000h).
\layout Description

F9-FA 'Portal' patch vector
\layout Description

FB-FC 'Room setup' patch vector
\layout Description

FD-FE 'Main loop' patch vector 
\layout Description

FF More flags:
\begin_deeper 
\layout Description

Bit\SpecialChar ~
0 set if Willy loses the items he's collected in the current room when
 he loses a life.
\layout Description

Bit\SpecialChar ~
1 set if escalators are down, 0 if up.
\layout Description

Bit\SpecialChar ~
2 set if ramps are escalators.
\layout Description

Bit\SpecialChar ~
3 set if Willy can fall any height in this room.
\layout Description

Bits\SpecialChar ~
4,5,6 reserved.
\layout Description

Bit\SpecialChar ~
7 set if this is a bonus room (each item gives Willy an extra life)
\end_deeper 
\layout Subsubsection

Bytes 100h onwards: Format V
\layout Description

100-13F Unused.
 Available to designer for portal graphic or patch vector(s).
\layout Description

140-1FF Room layout, 3 bits per cell.
\layout Subsubsection

Bytes 100h onwards: Format W
\layout Description

100-1FF Room layout, 4 bits per cell.
\layout Subsubsection

Bytes 100h onwards: Format X
\layout Description

100-33F Unused.
 Available to designer for portal graphic, patch vector(s), sprites, guardians,
 or all of the above.
\layout Description

340-3FF Room layout, 3 bits per cell.
\layout Subsubsection

Bytes 100h onwards: Format Y
\layout Description

100-2FF Unused.
 Available to designer for portal graphic, patch vector(s), sprites, guardians,
 or all of the above.
\layout Description

300-3FF Room layout, 4 bits per cell.
\layout Subsubsection

Bytes 100h onwards: Format Z
\layout Description

100-1FF Unused.
 Available to designer for portal graphic, patch vector(s), sprites or guardians.
\layout Description

200-3FF Room layout, 8 bits per cell.
 Unknown attributes take the 'water' graphic.
\layout Subsubsection

Bytes 100h onwards: Format [
\layout Description

100-2FF Unused.
 Available to designer for portal graphic, patch vector(s), sprites, guardians
 or all of the above.
\layout Description

300-3FF Room layout, 4 bits per cell.
\layout Subsection

Cell Types Table (formats W, Y, Z)
\layout Standard

In these formats, the meaning of each cell is determined globally rather
 than by room.
 There is a 16-byte table at F4FFh in bank 7.
 Each byte defines the meaning of a cell (so byte 0 refers to the first
 cell in the room definition, byte 1 to the second, and so on).
\layout Standard

Cell types are:
\layout Description

0 Air
\layout Description

1 Water
\layout Description

2 Earth
\layout Description

3 Fire
\layout Description

4 Ramp 
\backslash 

\layout Description

5 Ramp /
\layout Description

6 Conveyor <<
\layout Description

7 Conveyor >>
\layout Description

8 Crumbling floor
\layout Description

9 Trampoline
\layout Description

10 Trap
\layout Description

11-15 reserved.
 
\layout Standard

After these 16 bytes are the attributes and bitmaps for the three "global"
 cells -- the ones which are the same in every room.
\layout Subsection

Cell Types Table (format [)
\layout Standard

The cell type table is at F519h and is 16 bytes long.
 There are no global cell definitions.
\layout Subsection

JSW64 Guardians
\layout Standard

The guardians in JSW64 are based on the JSW128 guardians, but with a few
 added features.
 A guardian definition is 8 bytes long; I refer to them as GB0-GB7.
 
\layout Standard

The low 4 bits of GB0 give the guardian type.
 This is one of:
\layout Description

0 Blank
\layout Description

1 Horizontal
\layout Description

2 Vertical
\layout Description

3 Rope
\layout Description

4 Arrow
\layout Description

5 Diagonal 1
\layout Description

6 Diagonal 2
\layout Description

7 Vertical, colour-cycling 
\layout Description

8 Extended guardian.
 Top 4 bits give subtype:
\begin_deeper 
\layout Description

08: Skylab.
 Behaves like a vertical guardian, except that at the end of its travel
 it 'crashes' and then reappears 8 columns to the right (wrapping, of course).
 Other bytes as vertical guardian, except that byte 7 is 'crash' position
 and byte 6 is 'restart' position.
 So for an upward skylab, byte 7 must be less than byte 6.
\layout Description

18: Angry Eugene.
 Other bytes as vertical guardian, except that byte 1 is colour; high bits
 must be zero.
 An Angry Eugene goes to the end of his travel and stops dead.
\layout Description

28: Angry Eugene (colour-cycling based on air supply)
\layout Description

38: Angry Eugene (colour-cycling using JSW64 method).
\layout Description

88: Trigger.
\begin_deeper 
\layout Description

Byte\SpecialChar ~
0: 88h
\layout Description

Bytes\SpecialChar ~
1-2: Address of a flag.
 When this flag becomes zero, bytes 3-7 are applied to the next guardian
 in the table; and byte 0 of this guardian is cleared so it doesn't happen
 again.
\layout Description

Byte\SpecialChar ~
3: New byte 0 of next guardian.
\layout Description

Bytes\SpecialChar ~
4-7: New bytes 4-7 of next guardian.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout Description

98: Switch.
\begin_deeper 
\layout Description

Byte\SpecialChar ~
0: 98h.
\layout Description

Byte\SpecialChar ~
1: Ink.
\layout Description

Byte\SpecialChar ~
2: X-coordinate and frame.
 The top half of the guardian sprite will be the 'off' switch and the bottom
 half will be the 'on' switch.
\layout Description

Byte\SpecialChar ~
3: Y-coordinate.
\layout Description

Byte\SpecialChar ~
4: State.
 1=Off 0=On.
\layout Description

Byte\SpecialChar ~
5: Sprite page.
 
\layout Description

Byte\SpecialChar ~
6: If it's off and Willy hits it, change state to this.
\layout Description

Byte\SpecialChar ~
7: If it's on and Willy hits it, change state to this.
\end_deeper 
\layout Description

A8: Opening wall.
\begin_deeper 
\layout Description

Byte\SpecialChar ~
0: A8h.
\layout Description

Bytes\SpecialChar ~
1-2: Address of flag (same as for the Trigger).
\layout Description

Byte\SpecialChar ~
3: Number of vertical lines this will remove.
\layout Description

Byte\SpecialChar ~
4: State.
 Initialised by JSWED to 1.
 It will become 0 when all lines are removed.
\layout Description

Byte\SpecialChar ~
5: Number of lines removed so far.
 Initialised by JSWED to 0.
\layout Description

Byte\SpecialChar ~
6: X-coordinate of wall to open.
\layout Description

Byte\SpecialChar ~
7: Y-coordinate of wall to open.
\end_deeper 
\end_deeper 
\layout Description

9 - Horizontal, colour-cycling
\layout Description

A - Vertical
\layout Description

B - Reserved
\layout Description

C - Reserved
\layout Description

D - Diagonal 1, colour-cycling
\layout Description

E - Diagonal 2, colour-cycling
\layout Description

F - Vertical, colour-cycling
\layout Subsubsection

Horizontal and Diagonal Guardians
\layout Standard

These guardian types behave like the standard JSW48 horizontal guardian.
 
\layout Standard

Diagonal ones use GB4 as their vertical speed.
\layout Standard

The top 3 bits of GB7 have these meanings:
\layout Description

Bit\SpecialChar ~
5: Half speed.
 Guardian will only move every other frame.
\layout Description

Bit\SpecialChar ~
6: Half-speed ticker (used when the game is running).
 
\layout Description

Bit\SpecialChar ~
7: Double speed.
 Guardian will be animated twice per frame.
 This means you only get two animation images instead of four, but the guardian
 now goes twice as fast as Willy!
\layout Subsubsection

Ropes
\layout Standard

Set bit 6 of GB0 to have a stationary rope.
 JSW64 has been patched so that a rope definition fits in 8 bytes; a rope
 can be followed by other guardians than arrows and it won't corrupt them.
\layout Subsubsection

Arrows
\layout Standard

GB1 is the ink colour to use for the arrow.
 Bits 3-7 of this byte will also be ORed to the screen, so it's best to
 leave them as 0.
\layout Standard

GB3 is the bitmap for the middle line of the arrow.
\layout Subsection

Patch Vectors
\layout Standard

There are three patch vectors in the room data.
 In order, these are:
\layout Itemize

Portal vector.
 This is called when Willy enters a portal.
 The Patch Vector Jumpblock (below) provides two possible implementations
 of this; or you could write your own.
\layout Itemize

Note that when you return from the Portal vector, the game will assume you
 have moved to a new room and set things up accordingly.
 If you want Willy to stay in the same room, return with
\layout LyX-Code

POP BC
\layout LyX-Code

RET
\layout Itemize

Room Setup vector.
 This is called when Willy enters a new room, after the room attributes
 and bitmap patterns have been generated.
 This can be used to put custom graphics in a room (like the Final Barrier
 does in Manic Miner).
 The default implementation is just to return.
\layout Itemize

Main Loop vector.
 This is called in the main game loop, just after the screen has been drawn
 and just before Willy moves.
 It corresponds to the patch vector in 'Geoff Mode' JSW.
\layout Subsubsection

Patch Vector Jumpblock
\layout Standard

The following entry points are provided as helper functions for patches
 that you write.
 More may be added later.
 
\layout Standard

The jumpblock is at a fixed location, and this will stay fixed in future
 JSW64 versions.
\layout LyX-Code

869F RET 
\layout Standard

For rooms without patches, the Room Setup and Main Loop patch vectors should
 be set to this address.
\layout LyX-Code

86A0 JP portal_with_fx_and_air
\layout Standard

This is the default portal patch vector.
 It takes Willy to the location specified in the room's portal settings.
 The portal special effect occurs (as in Manic Miner); and if the room has
 a limited air supply, it will be run down to zero with a whining noise
 (again, as in Manic Miner).
\layout LyX-Code

86A3 JP portal_with_fx 
\layout Standard

As above, but does not do the 'air-running-down' thing.
\layout LyX-Code

86A6 JP portal_without_fx
\layout Standard

As above, but with no special effects at all (like the JSW128 teleporter).
\layout LyX-Code

86A9 JP ptl_fx
\layout Standard

This performs the portal special effect only.
\layout LyX-Code

86AC JP airend
\layout Standard

This performs the air-running-down-to-zero effect.
\layout LyX-Code

86AF JP ptl_both
\layout Standard

This performs the above two effects, one after the other.
\layout LyX-Code

86B2 RST 0
\layout Standard

The last entry in the jump block is always RST 0.
 If more entries are added, they will go at 86B2h and the RST 0 will move
 to the new end of the jump block.
\layout Subsubsection

Music Jumpblock
\layout Standard

The following entry points are provided as helper functions for patches
 that might want to amend the game music system.
\layout Standard

The jumpblock is at a fixed location, and this will stay fixed in future
 JSW64 versions.
 It is also present in JSW128 (HL9 and later).
\layout LyX-Code

FEC0 JP tuneoff 
\layout Standard

This will stop the in-game tune playing.
 Returns NZ if the tune was not playing, Z if it was.
\layout LyX-Code

FEC3 JP tuneon
\layout Standard

This will set the tune going.
 Note that the tune pointers at 0FE8Eh must have been set to point to the
 start of the selected tune.
\layout LyX-Code

FEC6 JP newtune
\layout Standard

Change the in-game tune.
 Enter with HL pointing to the 6-byte header of the new in-game tune.
 If no tune is playing, nothing obvious happens; if a tune is playing, it
 is stopped and the new tune starts.
\layout LyX-Code

FEC9 DEFS 6
\layout Standard

A copy of the 6-byte header of the last tune selected with NEWTUNE (FEC6).
 This is the tune that will be played when the current in-game tune comes
 to an end (used to make the tune into a continous loop)
\layout Subsection

Guardian Patch Vectors
\layout Standard

It is possible to define new guardian types, or change the behaviour of
 existing guardian types (JSW64:Manic Miner does, for example).
 The JSW64 engine supports up to 30 guardian types, the behaviour of which
 is defined in four tables:
\layout Description

84D2-84F1: Table of 16 movement routines, for guardian types 0-15 (types
 0 and 8 should not be altered).
 The movement routine updates the guardian's position and colour, but does
 not usually test for collisions.
 At the end of a movement routine, jump to the movement handler for type
 0; if you RETurn instead, guardians after your custom guardian will not
 move.
\layout Description

EC66-EC85: Table of 16 drawing routines, for guardian types 0-15 (types
 0 and 8 should not be altered).
 As with movement, you should jump to the type 0 drawing routine rather
 than RETurn.
 If Willy should lose a life, jump to MANDEAD1, 90B7h.
\layout Description

EC98-ECB7: Table of 16 movement routines for guardian types 08h, 18h, 28h...
 
\layout Description

ECB8-ECD7: Table of 16 drawing routines for guardian types 08h, 18h, 28h...
\layout Standard

A standard JSW64 game leaves guardian types 0Bh, 0Ch, 48h, 58h, 68h, 78h,
 D8h, E8h and F8h undefined.
 JSW64:Manic Miner defines 58h, 68h and D8h as custom guardian types, though
 only D8h is used in the game itself.
 
\layout Subsection

Memory Map
\layout Standard

This section lists memory areas or addresses that might be useful to a machine-c
ode programmer writing JSW64 patch vectors or other custom code.
 The list does not attempt to cover every change between JSW48 and JSW64.
\layout Standard

In normal use, memory bank 0 is present at 0C000h.
 The other banks are mainly used to store data, though some room-initialisation
 code is present in bank 7.
\layout Description

8200-82FF: In formats V and W, this memory is free for the game designer
 to put sprites or other data in (JSWED does not support this yet).
 In formats X, Y, Z and [ it contains the third page of the currently loaded
 room definition.
 Note that in formats X, Y and [, this is part of the 'free space' in the
 room, and may contain patch vectors, guardians or sprites.
\layout Description

8300-83FF: In formats V and W, this memory is free for the game designer
 to put sprites or other data in.
 In formats X, Y, Z and [ it contains the fourth page of the current room
 definition.
 In format X (but not Y, Z or [) the first 64 bytes of this page are part
 of the 'free space' in the room.
\layout Description

8553: Number of objects in the current room that haven't yet been collected.
 
\layout Description

85C9: Engine variant, 'V' to '['.
 Not used by the game, but essential for JSWED.
\layout Description

85CA: Number of items in the game (in JSW48 and JSW128 this is at A3FF).
\layout Description

8640-869E: Memory map, as in JSW128, but starts 12 bytes sooner.
\layout Description

87B2: Call a function in memory bank 7.
 Enter with HL=address of function.
 DE, IX and IY will be passed unchanged to the function; AF and BC are used
 by this routine.
\layout Description

8D33-8D52: Runtime cell type table - expanded from the table in the room
 definition (variants V,X) or the global table (other variants).
 Two bytes per cell type; the first is the attribute, and the second is
 the behaviour (0=Air, 1=Water etc.).
 This could be changed by patch vector code at run time.
 Note that in variants V and X, only the first 8 entries in the table are
 used.
\layout Description

97E0-97FF: The attributes used to draw the 'air remaining' bar.
\layout Description

C000-C0FF: In formats V and W, contains object locations.
 In variants X, Y, Z and [ this is unused.
 It's possible to extend the font into this area if you want characters
 0-31 to be printable.
\layout Description

C100-C3FF: The font.
\layout Subsubsection

Banks 1,3,4,6
\layout Standard

These memory banks hold the room definitions.
 Bank 1 holds the first 32 rooms (V and W); or the first 16 rooms (other
 variants).
 Then Bank 3 holds the next 32/16 rooms, and so on.
\layout Subsubsection

Bank 7 memory map
\layout Description

C000-D5FF: Title screen
\layout Description

D600-DAFF: Scrolly message
\layout Description

DB00-DB05: Bank 7 jumpblock (JSW64 internal, unlikely to be useful in patch
 vectors).
\layout Description

DB06-DD93: Room decompression code (size varies, depending which variant
 we're talking about)
\layout Description

DD94-E2FE: Reserved
\layout Description

E2FF-F4FE: The two 'top third' screens.
 Each is formed of 2k bitmap and 256 bytes attributes.
\layout Description

F4FF-F529: Cell map and global cell definitions
\layout Description

F52A-FFFF: Title screen music
\the_end
